---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import TopBar from '../../components/TopBar.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import CTASection from '../../components/CTASection.astro';

const publishedDate = '2026-06-03';
const articleTitle = "Bill 60 Ontario: Tenants + Landlords must know";
const articleDescription = 'A plain-language guide to Ontario\'s Bill 60. Four rule changes for renting, side-by-side before/after, and what to do if you\'re a tenant or landlord.';
const articleUrl = 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/bill-60-ontario';

const changes = [
  {
    n: 1,
    label: 'Behind on rent? Less time to pay.',
    old: 'You had 14 days to pay before the landlord could file with the LTB.',
    new: 'You now have only 7 days.',
    helps: 'Landlord',
  },
  {
    n: 2,
    label: 'Landlord moving in? No more month\'s rent back.',
    old: 'If the landlord made you leave for personal use (N12), they paid you one month\'s rent.',
    new: 'If the landlord gives 120 days\' notice, no payment is owed. A 60-day notice still requires the payment.',
    helps: 'Landlord',
  },
  {
    n: 3,
    label: 'Maintenance complaints at a rent hearing now cost money up front.',
    old: 'You could raise repair problems as a defense at a non-payment hearing for free.',
    new: 'You must first pay 50% of the arrears the landlord claims, before the hearing, to raise those issues.',
    helps: 'Landlord',
  },
  {
    n: 4,
    label: 'Less time to ask the LTB to review its order.',
    old: 'You had 30 days to request a review.',
    new: 'You have 15 days (the LTB can still allow more time in some cases).',
    helps: 'Landlord',
  },
];

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline: articleTitle,
  description: articleDescription,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: 'Peter Law', url: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/about' },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Peter Law, Toronto Real Estate',
    logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/photos/peter-cn-tower.jpg', width: 1500, height: 1887 },
  },
  datePublished: publishedDate,
  dateModified: '2026-11-26',
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': articleUrl },
  keywords: 'Bill 60 Ontario, Fighting Delays Building Faster Act, Ontario eviction rules 2026, N12 compensation Bill 60, fixed-term lease Ontario, Toronto landlord tenant Bill 60, Residential Tenancies Act 2026, Ontario rent arrears hearing changes',
};

export const prerender = true;

const hreflangAlternates = [
  { hreflang: 'en-CA', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/bill-60-ontario' },
  { hreflang: 'zh-CN', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/chinese/blog/bill-60-ontario' },
  { hreflang: 'fr-CA', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/french/blog/bill-60-ontario' },
  { hreflang: 'x-default', href: 'https://www.realtorpeterlaw.com/blog/bill-60-ontario' },
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${articleTitle} | Peter Law`}
  description={articleDescription}
  bodyClass="guide-page article-page"
  hreflangAlternates={hreflangAlternates}
>
  <TopBar />
  <Header active="blog" />

  <article>
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="eyebrow"><a href="/blog" class="back-link"><svg width="11" height="11" viewBox="0 0 14 14" fill="none" style="vertical-align:-1px;margin-right:5px;" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 7H1M6 12L1 7l5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>Blog</a> · Legislation</div>
        <h1>{articleTitle}</h1>
        <p class="article-lead">Ontario passed a new law in late 2025 called Bill 60. It changes the rules for renting. Most changes help landlords. Tenants have less time and less power. Here's what changed, in plain words.</p>
        <div class="article-byline">
          <span>By <a href="/about">Peter Law</a></span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <time datetime={publishedDate}>June 3, 2026</time>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>6 min read</span>
          <span class="dot">·</span>
          <span>Updated November 26, 2026</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="major" style="padding-top:32px;">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="prose-grid">
          <aside class="prose-side">
            <h2 class="side-h">On this page</h2>
            <ul>
              <li><a href="#what">What is Bill 60?</a></li>
              <li><a href="#diagram">Old rules vs new rules</a></li>
              <li><a href="#five">The 4 changes explained</a></li>
              <li><a href="#status">Is it in force now?</a></li>
              <li><a href="#tenants">If you're a tenant</a></li>
              <li><a href="#landlords">If you're a landlord</a></li>
              <li><a href="#why">Why this matters</a></li>
            </ul>
          </aside>

          <div class="prose-body">
            <h2 id="what">What is Bill 60?</h2>
            <p>Bill 60's full name is the <em>Fighting Delays, Building Faster Act, 2025</em>. The government's word for it is "omnibus": that just means one law that changes many other laws. Bill 60 changes 16 laws at once.</p>
            <p>The government says it has two goals:</p>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li>Speed up new housing construction.</li>
              <li>Speed up the Landlord and Tenant Board (LTB). The LTB is the court that handles renting disputes.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>To do this, Bill 60 shortens timelines and removes some of the steps tenants used to have. Most of the rule changes help landlords.</p>

            <p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Bill 60 is a new Ontario law. It changes the rules for renting. It makes things faster and easier for landlords. It gives tenants less time and less protection. The law passed on November 24, 2025. Its renting changes took effect in 2026: the shorter review deadline on July 1, and the rest on September 21. Your lease still rolls over month to month when it ends; that did not change.</p>

            <div class="b60-stats" role="group" aria-label="Bill 60 at a glance: the four numbers that changed">
              <div class="b60-stat">
                <div class="b60-stat-num">7</div>
                <div class="b60-stat-cap">Days to pay before an N4 hits the LTB <span>(was 14)</span></div>
              </div>
              <div class="b60-stat">
                <div class="b60-stat-num">15</div>
                <div class="b60-stat-cap">Days to ask the LTB to review an order <span>(was 30)</span></div>
              </div>
              <div class="b60-stat">
                <div class="b60-stat-num">50<span class="b60-stat-pct">%</span></div>
                <div class="b60-stat-cap">Of claimed arrears to pay before the hearing to raise other issues</div>
              </div>
              <div class="b60-stat">
                <div class="b60-stat-num">120</div>
                <div class="b60-stat-cap">Days notice an N12 needs to skip the one-month payment</div>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="callout"><strong>Heads up:</strong> Bill 60 is the biggest change to Ontario renting law in 10 years. The rules from a year ago are not the rules today. Always confirm with the LTB or a paralegal before you serve a notice or respond to one.</div>

            <h2 id="diagram">Old rules vs. new rules, side by side</h2>
            <p>Four things changed. Here they are at a glance.</p>

            <div class="b60-intro">Four rules changed. Three cut tenant time or protections. One removes a payment landlords used to owe. Quick-scan view below; full explanations follow.</div>

            <div class="b60-table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Table">
              <table class="b60-table">
                <thead>
                  <tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Before Bill 60</th><th scope="col">After Bill 60</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {changes.map((c) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td class="b60-rule">{c.label}</td>
                      <td class="b60-before">{c.old}</td>
                      <td class="b60-after">{c.new}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>

            <h2 id="five">The 4 changes explained</h2>

            <details class="b60-change">
              <summary class="b60-change-summary">1. Behind on rent? You have less time to catch up.</summary>
              <div class="b60-change-body">
                <p>If you don't pay rent, your landlord sends you a form called an N4. It tells you to pay or move out.</p>
              <p>Before: you had 14 days. Now: you have only 7 days.</p>
              <p>What to do: set up automatic rent payments. The grace period is now half what it used to be. One missed reminder can start the eviction clock.</p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="b60-change">
              <summary class="b60-change-summary">2. The "I'm moving in" eviction is now cheaper for landlords.</summary>
              <div class="b60-change-body">
                <p>An N12 notice is when the landlord (or a buyer, or a close family member) wants to live in the home themselves. The landlord has to give you written notice and let you move out.</p>
              <p>Before: the landlord had to pay you one month's rent as a thank-you for moving. Or offer you another similar place to live.</p>
              <p>Now: if the landlord gives you 120 days' notice (4 months), they don't have to pay you. A 60-day N12 still includes the one month payment.</p>
              <p>What to do: if you get an N12, don't assume it's real. Fake N12s are the most common landlord scam. A paralegal review costs less than losing your home. If proven fake at the LTB, the landlord can be fined up to $50,000.</p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="b60-change">
              <summary class="b60-change-summary">3. Raising maintenance complaints at a rent hearing now costs money up front.</summary>
              <div class="b60-change-body">
                <p>A common move: a tenant is behind on rent. The landlord takes them to the LTB. At the hearing, the tenant says "I shouldn't have to pay full rent because the apartment has problems." This often slowed things down.</p>
              <p>Before: tenants could raise maintenance issues as a free defense.</p>
              <p>Now: for applications filed on or after September 21, 2026, the tenant must first pay 50% of the arrears the landlord claims, before the hearing, to raise those issues.</p>
              <p>What to do: if you have real maintenance problems, document them in real time. Send your landlord written requests. Take dated photos. Don't wait for a hearing to start collecting proof.</p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="b60-change">
              <summary class="b60-change-summary">4. Less time to ask for a review of an LTB order.</summary>
              <div class="b60-change-body">
                <p>If you disagree with an LTB order, you can ask the LTB to review it.</p>
              <p>Before: 30 days. Now: 15 days. The LTB can still give more time if it is fair in the circumstances.</p>
              <p>What to do: if you get a decision you don't agree with, find a paralegal that same week. Don't wait.</p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <div class="callout"><strong>What did NOT change: your lease still rolls over.</strong> An early version of the plan would have ended the automatic switch to month-to-month when a fixed-term lease ends. That idea was dropped. When your lease ends, it still continues month to month on the same terms. You do not have to sign a new lease or move out, and rent increases still follow the usual rules (once every 12 months, with 90 days' written notice).</div>

            <h2 id="status">Is Bill 60 in force now?</h2>
            <p>Yes. Bill 60 passed on November 24, 2025, and its renting changes started in two steps in 2026:</p>
            <ul class="bullets">
              <li><strong>July 1, 2026:</strong> 15 days (not 30) to ask the LTB to review an order.</li>
              <li><strong>September 21, 2026:</strong> the 7-day N4, the 50% payment before raising other issues at a rent hearing, and the 120-day N12 with no compensation.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>The new rules apply to notices served and applications filed on or after those dates.</p>
            <div class="callout"><strong>Before you do anything:</strong> use the LTB's current forms. An outdated form or a wrong date can get your whole case thrown out. Tenants, same thing. If a notice looks weird or rushed, get advice before assuming it's valid.</div>

            <details class="b60-section-toggle" id="tenants">
              <summary class="b60-section-toggle-summary">If you're a tenant in Toronto</summary>
              <ul class="bullets">
                <li><strong>Turn on auto-pay for rent.</strong> The 7-day N4 window is short. Don't let a forgotten payment start an eviction.</li>
                <li><strong>Your lease still rolls over.</strong> When your fixed term ends, you can stay month to month on the same terms. You don't have to sign a new lease.</li>
                <li><strong>Keep maintenance evidence.</strong> Write your requests in email or text. Save photos with dates. You may need them.</li>
                <li><strong>An N12 is not always real.</strong> If you get one, talk to a paralegal. Common ones cost $200–$500 to review.</li>
                <li><strong>If you get a bad decision from the LTB, move fast.</strong> 15 days is short.</li>
              </ul>
            </details>

            <details class="b60-section-toggle" id="landlords">
              <summary class="b60-section-toggle-summary">If you're a landlord in Toronto</summary>
              <ul class="bullets">
                <li><strong>Use the current LTB forms and dates.</strong> The new rules apply only to notices served and applications filed on or after their start dates. A wrong form can blow up your case.</li>
                <li><strong>Document like you might end up at the LTB tomorrow.</strong> Keep maintenance records, tenant emails, condition reports.</li>
                <li><strong>Fixed-term leases still roll over.</strong> Bill 60 did not change this. At the end of the term, the tenancy continues month to month unless it ends lawfully.</li>
                <li><strong>The 120-day N12 with no payment is only worth using if your case is honest.</strong> Fake N12s now draw $50,000+ in penalties at the LTB.</li>
              </ul>
            </details>

            <h2 id="why">Why this matters</h2>
            <p>Bill 60 is the most landlord-friendly Ontario renting law in over a decade. The government's bet is that easier eviction rules will get more landlords to rent out empty units. That should mean more rental supply.</p>
            <p>Critics say the law removes tenant protections without solving the deeper problem, that rent is too expensive.</p>
            <p>Both can be partly true. What matters today is knowing the rules of the game you're now playing.</p>

            <p class="legal-note">This article is information only, not legal advice. Bill 60 is complex, and the LTB's forms and rules can change. For your situation, talk to a paralegal registered with the Law Society of Ontario, contact the LTB, or visit a community legal clinic. Sources used to build this article: Tribunals Ontario implementation notes, Canadian Centre for Housing Rights, Globe and Mail coverage of the bill, OntarioLandlord.ca implementation tracker. Updated November 26, 2026 to reflect what came into force and to correct an earlier statement about fixed-term leases. I'm a registered real estate Broker, not a lawyer.</p>

            <div class="seo-tags" role="group" aria-label="Keywords">
              <h2 class="seo-tags-h">Keywords</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Bill 60 Ontario</li>
                <li>Fighting Delays Building Faster Act</li>
                <li>Ontario eviction rules 2026</li>
                <li>N12 compensation Bill 60</li>
                <li>N4 7-day notice Ontario</li>
                <li>Fixed-term lease Ontario</li>
                <li>LTB review 15 days</li>
                <li>Residential Tenancies Act 2026</li>
                <li>Toronto landlord tenant law</li>
                <li>Bill 60 tenant rights</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

  <CTASection heading="Got a Bill 60 question?" lead="Tenant who just got a notice, or a landlord trying to figure out what's in force? Send me what you have. I'll tell you what I can, and refer you to a paralegal I trust if you need more." />
  <Footer />

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(jsonLd)} />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .b60-stats { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 0; margin: 24px 0 28px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; overflow: hidden; background: var(--bg-warm); }
  .b60-stat { padding: 22px 16px; text-align: center; border-right: 1px solid var(--rule); display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; justify-content: flex-start; gap: 10px; }
  .b60-stat:last-child { border-right: none; }
  .b60-stat-num { font-family: 'Fraunces', Georgia, serif; font-weight: 400; font-size: 44px; line-height: 1; color: var(--ink); letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .b60-stat-pct { font-size: 28px; color: var(--ink-soft); margin-left: 1px; }
  .b60-stat-cap { font-size: 12px; line-height: 1.45; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: 200px; }
  .b60-stat-cap span { color: var(--ink-mute); }
  @media (max-width: 720px) {
    .b60-stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; }
    .b60-stat { border-right: 1px solid var(--rule); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .b60-stat:nth-child(2) { border-right: none; }
    .b60-stat:nth-child(3), .b60-stat:nth-child(4) { border-bottom: none; }
    .b60-stat-num { font-size: 36px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 420px) {
    .b60-stats { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .b60-stat { border-right: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .b60-stat:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  }
  .b60-intro { background: var(--bg-warm); border-left: 3px solid var(--ink); padding: 18px 22px; margin: 24px 0 28px; border-radius: 4px; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: 1.65; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .b60-intro strong { color: var(--ink); }
  .b60-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin: 0 0 32px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 6px; }
  .b60-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; min-width: 600px; }
  .b60-table thead th { background: var(--ink); color: var(--surface); padding: 14px 16px; text-align: left; font-weight: 500; font-size: 12px; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .b60-table tbody td { padding: 16px; vertical-align: top; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); line-height: 1.55; }
  .b60-table tbody tr:nth-child(even) td { background: var(--bg-warm); }
  .b60-table .b60-rule { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); width: 26%; font-size: 14px; }
  .b60-table .b60-before { color: var(--ink-soft); width: 37%; }
  .b60-table .b60-after { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; width: 37%; }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .b60-table-wrap { border-radius: 4px; }
    .b60-table thead th { font-size: 11px; padding: 12px 14px; }
    .b60-table tbody td { padding: 14px; font-size: 13.5px; }
  }
</style>
